[{:db/id 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2a7fbace-2464-5d79-be05-e6f28745c3de"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2a7fbace-2464-5d79-be05-e6f28745c3de" :question/id #uuid "2a7fbace-2464-5d79-be05-e6f28745c3de" :question/math-academy-id "q-178014" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=12-12=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})$ is not a unit vector since\n\n$$\n‖Q({u}_{1})‖=‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-288b1ab6-6f15-58b3-8714-e1582f968b3b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-288b1ab6-6f15-58b3-8714-e1582f968b3b" :answer-field/id #uuid "288b1ab6-6f15-58b3-8714-e1582f968b3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ac673688-1d30-5b45-8f99-0b4b41a43e4a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ac673688-1d30-5b45-8f99-0b4b41a43e4a" "answer-5fce5c53-c88f-5213-9b57-f76cc256d9ec" "answer-a91a4b12-8794-5dbc-b111-cafaa0420df7" "answer-75a2a860-9b99-512b-901f-14c2d162a694" "answer-87e451be-c0a6-5f4f-8367-e5f2bd3d8579"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac673688-1d30-5b45-8f99-0b4b41a43e4a" :answer/id #uuid "ac673688-1d30-5b45-8f99-0b4b41a43e4a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fce5c53-c88f-5213-9b57-f76cc256d9ec" :answer/id #uuid "5fce5c53-c88f-5213-9b57-f76cc256d9ec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a91a4b12-8794-5dbc-b111-cafaa0420df7" :answer/id #uuid "a91a4b12-8794-5dbc-b111-cafaa0420df7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75a2a860-9b99-512b-901f-14c2d162a694" :answer/id #uuid "75a2a860-9b99-512b-901f-14c2d162a694" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87e451be-c0a6-5f4f-8367-e5f2bd3d8579" :answer/id #uuid "87e451be-c0a6-5f4f-8367-e5f2bd3d8579" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-e4410405-361b-5f52-bbe2-fae1dae52eb7" :activity/id #uuid "e4410405-361b-5f52-bbe2-fae1dae52eb7" :activity/title "Orthogonal Linear Transformations" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050910 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-721b390a-ee2e-5f0b-9e0f-16ccdf1c5e8b" "step-2a7a4301-aad4-5d08-a205-223ae4b21b05" "step-ff6d9303-29e4-5651-9e3f-2878730378dc" "step-81b7f578-5735-5cd8-9573-a001cb417b38" "step-99